.TH PUTS 3  "May 15, 1985"
.AT 3
.SH NAME
puts, fputs \- put a string on a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <stdio.h>

puts(\fIchar *s\fP)

fputs(\fI char *s\fP, \fIFILE *stream\fP)
.ft R
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B puts
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the standard output stream
.B stdout
and appends a
newline character.
.PP
.B fputs
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the named output
.IR stream .
.PP
Neither routine copies the terminal null character.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fopen(3), gets(3), putc(3), printf(3), ferror(3), fread(3), fwrite(3)
.SH BUGS
.B puts
appends a newline,
.B fputs
does not, all in the name of backward compatibility.
